[Context](https://rust-lang.zulipchat.com/#narrow/stream/187312-wg-async/topic/future.20sizes).

Recently, generator lowering was moved from running after a subset of optimizations to running strictly before all optimizations. At the time, this was done because of concerns about soundness. However, the intention was never for that change to be permanent. I had assumed that it would be some years before the change was undone, as it required spec support that I did not think anyone was working on, but because of renewed interest in this topic, there was progress on that front and I actually think that a change to move generator lowering back after most optimizations might be reasonable for the near-ish future (weeks to months).

Unfortunately, this PR as it stands is fairly incompatible with that. It makes the set of locals that is live across yield points at the time of MIR generator lowering observable to users. This means we can't run optimizations before that points, as they'll be user-visible. For example, if the user writes
```rust
async {
    let v = Vec::<i32>::new();
    foo().await;
    dbg!(v);
}
```

The future will be `Send` as the only local live across a yield point is a `Vec<i32>`. However, if SROA runs then there will be a `*mut i32` live across the yield point and the future will no longer be `Send`.

This is issue is fairly fundamental - if we want do do better optimizations on futures, there will always need to be two computations of the generator locals - one for the user-visible effects that needs to be forwards compatible, and one for computing the actual runtime layout that should be as optimized as possible. I therefore think that wanting to have only a single computation of this information in the long-term ought to be a non-goal.

That being said, I don't think this PR is necessarily wrong. It still seems reasonable to want to do both these computations on MIR. My question is now as follows: If we want to do pre-generator lowering optimizations in the near-ish future, what is the difficulty of separating the computation of the generator layout at runtime from the computation of upvar types that affect the typeck behavior?

