No they don't. The inner types being `!Unpin` have no relation to the properties of the combinator. The combinator can pin all values on the heap, for all the users care.

The only thing that `!Unpin` guarantees is that `Pin<P>` cannot be safely turned into `P`. If the combinator doesn't make a `Pin<P>`, it has no reason to worry about `!Unpin` on its fields. If the user considers it very important that their type remains pinned, they should pin it before using the combinator.

