As I understand it, the whole difference here comes down to whether the `poll_fn` closure can consider its `!Unpin` captures to be pinned from the time the closure is first called to the time the closure is dropped.

If `PollFn<F>: Unpin` for all `F`, then the `poll_fn` closure can never make this guarantee. The `PollFn` can always be unpinned and moved between `poll()` calls, moving all of the captures with it. Therefore, it is always unsound for the closure to call `Pin::new_unchecked(&mut capture)`, unless it has strict control over all the users of the `PollFn`.

If `PollFn<F>: Unpin` iff `F: Unpin`, then the closure can now make this guarantee, since users can no longer move the `PollFn` between `poll()` calls. It still has the footgun that code inside the closure can move the captures. However, this is a far smaller footgun, since the closure has full control over its captures! In fact, it seems exactly equivalent to the already existing pinned-`Drop` footgun: structurally pinned fields could be safely moved from the `&mut self`, so the implementer has a soundness requirement not to do that. (Indeed, the common advice is to only work with `unsafe { Pin::new_unchecked(self) }`.)

This leads to a sound pattern of pinning any structural fields on entry to the `poll_fn` closure:
```rust
use std::{
    future::{self, Future},
    pin::Pin,
    task::{Context, Poll},
};

pub fn example<T, R, F>(mut value: T, mut f: F) -> impl Future<Output = R>
where
    F: FnMut(Pin<&mut T>) -> R,
{
    future::poll_fn(move |_: &mut Context<'_>| { // captures `value`, `f`
        // SAFETY:
        // If `T: Unpin`, this is trivially sound.
        // Otherwise, if `PollFn<Closure>: Unpin`, this is UNSOUND! ⚠
        // Otherwise, if `PollFn<Closure>: !Unpin`, `value` cannot be moved
        // between calls to this closure, since `poll()` requires a
        // `Pin<&mut PollFn<Closure>>`. Furthermore, `value` is not moved within
        // this closure, since the variable is not used except to pin it (and to
        // implicitly drop it once this closure is dropped). Therefore, the
        // pinning guarantees are upheld, and this call is sound.
        let pinned_value = unsafe { Pin::new_unchecked(&mut value) };
        Poll::Ready(f(pinned_value))
    })
}
```

Thus, making `PollFn<F>: !Unpin` when `F: !Unpin` makes `poll_fn` strictly more powerful, if I am not missing anything. With this change, `poll_fn` closures would have feature parity with ordinary poll functions, which can access structurally pinned fields in the same way that closures would be able to access structurally pinned captures.

The primary difference is that the pinned captures can be unsoundly accessed by safe code within the closure. However, this footgun can be easily mitigated by simply not using the captures without pinning them, much in the same way that structs must pin `&mut self` in their `Drop` impls. Meanwhile, with the current `Unpin` status quo, no amount of care by the closure author can prevent the captures from being moved. I simply do not see why this unconditional `Unpin` impl should exist, apart from the difficulty of backporting the change at this point.

**EDIT:** Now I see the issue: even if `poll_fn` promises that its `Future` impl does nothing but call `FnMut::call_mut()` on the closure, we don't guarantee that the language itself doesn't move the captures before entering or after exiting the body of the closure. This doesn't seem like a particularly difficult guarantee, unless we plan on changing the functionality of closures in the future. We would be making this guarantee if #103396 were merged.

Given that the (/a?) underlying problem is "pinning a variable to the closure's captures is only sound if the closure's captures have a stable location" could there be a model where we allow code authors to request that the closure's capture storage _is_ stable?  Something similar to the `move` modifier on closure definitions:

```
let future = Future::new();

// `pinned` keyword creates a "closure with stable storage for captures" hence the `Pin::new_unchecked` is safe
let f = pinned move | | { Pin::new_unchecked(future); ... };
```

Then we can introduce two new traits (which @afetisov suggested in the IRLO thread):

```
// This is "FnMut" for "Pin<Self>"
trait FnPinnedMut<Args> {
    type Output;
    extern "rust-call" fn call_pinned_mut(Pin<&mut Self>, args: Args) -> Self::Output;
}

// This is "Fn" for "Pin<Self>"
trait FnPinned<Args> {
    type Output;
    extern "rust-call" fn call_pinned(Pin<&Self>, args: Args) -> Self::Output;
}
```

Now, if the author requests a `pinned` closure they get a closure that implements `FnPinned(Mut)` instead of `Fn(mut)` and probably `!Unpin` based on the current rules for capturing `!Unpin` values (it could theoretically implement `Unpin` if the captures are all `Unpin` - though this would likely be a good lint for "unnecessarily pinned closure").  In such a closure, it is safe/sound to `Pin::new_unchecked` any of the captured variables (this sidesteps the "which fields do I project?" problem by making the closure author chose which captures to actually pin) since to call the closure it must be pinned, implying the storage is stable across this and all subsequent calls.  Note that such a closure does _not_ implement `Fn(Mut)` in order to force the caller to `Pin` before the first call of the closure body.

Arguably, this model breaks pinning rules, since the captures are under a pin (the one around the closure) but can be accessed without a pin (from within the closure body).  Fortunately closure captures can't be seen from outside the closure body so there's no way for anything to access or move a value they see as pinned (outside sees a pinned black box, inside sees an unpinned set of values which are in stable storage and hence can be marked as pinned soundly - at which point, they can no longer be moved).  In other words, the outer `Pin` guarantees that the capture storage doesn't move and the inner one(s) guarantee that some values _stored in that storage_ don't move.

This ^^ feels like a valid (and valuable) relaxation of the pinning rules though I'd love to hear why it's not 🙉.

This proposal would mean that "Pinned Closures" can't be used in today's `poll_fn` (which requires that the closure be `Fn(Mut)`) but, since they are opt-in, that's not a breaking change, and it would be easy to implement a new `poll_fn` that works for pinned and non-pinned closures and be `Unpin` if the inner function doesn't need stable storage (and hence can be moved between calls).

```
pub struct PollFn<F> {
    f: F, // The inner closure is structural for pinning
}

// `PollFn` is `Unpin` if the inner function can be called unpinned (and hence we don't care if it gets moved)
impl<T, F> Unpin for PollFn<F> 
where
    F: FnMut(&mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<T>
{ }

// `poll_fn` can poll pinned closures as well as unpinned ones
pub fn poll_fn<T, F>(f: F) -> PollFn<F>
where
    F: FnPinnedMut(&mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<T>,
{
    PollFn { f }
}

impl<T, F> Future for PollFn<F>
where
    F: FnPinnedMut(&mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<T>,
{
    type Output = T;

    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<T> {
        self.map_unchecked_mut(|s| &mut s.f).call_pinned_mut(cx)
    }
}
```

As far as I can see it would be sound to implement `FnPinned(Mut)` for closures which today are `Fn(Mut)` (since if I can call a closure unpinned then, given the above relaxation to the rules, I can also do so pinned as the body of the closure sees the captures unpinned in both cases and it's unsound for such a closure to pin them).  This also means that functions (as opposed to closures) are `FnPinned(Mut)` for free which is nice for ergonomics.

> The primary difference is that the pinned captures can be unsoundly accessed by safe code within the closure.

FWIW, *this is incorrect* — or at least, misleading. Pinned captures cannot be unsoundly accessed by the exclusive use of safe code in the closure; `Pin::new_unchecked` needs to be used to project the pin onto the captures, as in a `Pin<&mut Self>` method.

Given `PollFn<impl !Unpin>: !Unpin`, it is *not automatically the case* that the closures' captures are themselves considered pinned.

To make this clear, here's an example ignoring the `Fn` traits:

```rust
struct PollFn<_> {
    inner: MyClosure {
        capture: MyState,
    },
}

// roughly PollFn: Unpin
fn PollFn::poll(&mut self, cx: _) -> _ {
    let inner: &mut MyClosure = &mut self.inner;
    MyClosure::poll(inner, cx)
}

// roughly PollFn: !Unpin
fn PollFn::poll(Pin<&mut self>, cx: _) -> _ {
    let inner: Pin<&mut MyClosure> = pin_project!(self.inner);
    MyClosure::poll(inner, cx)
}
```

It is the case that we're effectively considering `MyClosure` structurally pinned — it's "just" that despite this we are calling it with the `Pin::get_mut_unchecked` plain `&mut` due to passing through the `FnMut` interface. Because closures do not actually expose the closure `self`, this is perfectly sound under this interpretation[^1].

[^1]: Important addition: we never actually construct a pinned view of `MyClosure` in the actual code, and just stay in `&mut`-land. Making a temporary `Pin` would make the implementation identical to this model, but it does not invalidate pin projection to not construct the intermediate `Pin`ned views. Additionally, not actually making the `Pin` has the important quality that we're leaving whether `PollFn.inner` is actually structurally pinned up to `MyClosure`; this becomes *essential* if it's ever made possible to implement `FnMut` directly, getting access to an unguarded `&mut MyClosure`.

The important observation here though is that we're only in charge of structurally pinning `PollFn.inner`. `MyClosure` is in charge of whether `MyClosure.capture` is structurally pinned, so it can soundly use either `Pin::new_unchecked(&mut self.capture)` ***exclusive-or*** `&mut self.capture`.

The caveat to this, of course, the exclusive-or. I enthusiastically agree that offering the option of structural pinning in `poll_fn` is the correct choice, but here's my analysis of the caveat.

<details><summary>poll_fn pinning caveat</summary>

Because a `poll_fn` is called multiple times, it's possible to write

```rust
poll_fn(|cx| {
    &mut capture;
    Pin::new_unchecked(&mut capture);

    // ...
})
```

which is not *immediately* obvious as being an issue, since `capture` is used by `&mut` before `Pin::new_unchecked` is written, thus before it's observed behind a pinned reference for any one call to the poll fn.

HOWEVER, importantly, this ***is*** necessarily an ***entirely localized*** condition. In order to use `capture`, the closure itself must itself name `capture` and decide by what reference kind it is used. The proof burden of `Pin::new_unchecked` is nearly identical to using it anywhere else — in fact, it is exactly identical to a `Poll<&mut self>` function where the first thing you do is `let Self { $(fields),* } = Pin::get_mut_unchecked(self)`, because that's actually ***exactly semantically equivalent***.

----
</details>

My conclusion here is that the caveats to `Pin::new_unchecked` in `poll_fn` (given this PR providing permission for structural pinning) are essentially the same as when using it for manual pin projection in a `Pin<&mut Self>` method. It's surely unfortunate that the interface can't easily be made safe[^2], but the soundness burden is something users of `Pin::new_unchecked` are already familiar with.

[^2]: Extremely untested and unproven, but you probably can reduce the room for error by providing a macro `poll!(unsafe move pin[$captures] |$cx| { $body })` expanding to `move |$cx| { let $captures = unsafe { Pin::new_unchecked(&mut $captures) }; $body }`. Then this could be made safe by forcing it's immediate passing to `poll_fn`, e.g. `poll_fn!($tts) = poll_fn(poll!(unsafe $tts))`.

